== fresh Postgres with databases events, keys, kms (docker compose down -v && up) ==
 Container 18-crypto-shredding-postgres-1 Healthy 
setup: databases events (append-only events), keys (per-subject wrapped DEKs, email blind index), kms (KEK 1, blind-index key)
== demo: two customers, PII encrypted under per-subject keys ==

## 1. One data key per subject, wrapped by the KEK
   concept: each customer gets a random 256-bit DEK; the key store keeps it only wrapped (encrypted) by a KEK held in the KMS
   alice -> subject ab06b94c-cc17-4c6b-95a7-b8ba1b2fb5ca, DEK wrapped by KEK 1
   bob   -> subject f78c088d-1d27-415f-814b-a03259492dbb, DEK wrapped by KEK 1
   check ok: the key store holds one wrapped DEK per subject, never the plaintext key

## 2. PII encrypted, the rest in clear
   concept: name, email and address are AES-256-GCM ciphertext under the subject's DEK; event type, order and amount stay queryable
   events <- #1 ab06b94c CustomerRegistered {} encrypted: name, email
   events <- #2 f78c088d CustomerRegistered {} encrypted: name, email
   events <- #3 ab06b94c OrderPlaced {"order":"A-1","amount":"42.50"} encrypted: ship_to
   events <- #4 f78c088d OrderPlaced {"order":"B-1","amount":"19.90"} encrypted: ship_to
   events <- #5 ab06b94c OrderPlaced {"order":"A-2","amount":"7.00"} encrypted: ship_to
   check ok: the events table holds no plaintext PII, but the amounts are in clear

## 3. Lookup by email through a blind index
   concept: ciphertext cannot be indexed, so the key store keeps HMAC(blind-index key, normalized email) -> subject
   " Alice@Example.COM " -> hmac 5a74a26e99651f14... -> subject ab06b94c-cc17-4c6b-95a7-b8ba1b2fb5ca
   #1 ab06b94c CustomerRegistered                          | name=Alice Martin email=alice@example.com
   #3 ab06b94c OrderPlaced        order=A-1 amount=42.50   | ship_to=12 rue des Lilas, Lyon
   #5 ab06b94c OrderPlaced        order=A-2 amount=7.00    | ship_to=12 rue des Lilas, Lyon
   check ok: a messy spelling of alice's email finds her subject through the blind index; her 3 events decrypt

## 4. A fresh IV for every encryption
   concept: same key, same plaintext, different ciphertext: equal values cannot be spotted, and GCM never reuses a nonce under one key
   seal("alice@example.com") #1 = vDHJu+UWoNEdlNpQoXl3Qu48OBeAy4OS...
   seal("alice@example.com") #2 = dU9yx7jxUvXjk1ky0zoDVb3RFifaWqjx...
   check ok: sealing the same value twice gives two different ciphertexts

## 5. AAD binds each ciphertext to its subject and field
   concept: the AAD (subject id + field name) is authenticated, not stored: a ciphertext moved elsewhere, or edited, fails to decrypt
   alice's email, read as alice's email: ok, "alice@example.com"
   alice's email ciphertext, pasted into her name field: rejected, Unsupported state or unable to authenticate data
   bob's email ciphertext, pasted into alice's email (wrong DEK and wrong AAD): rejected, Unsupported state or unable to authenticate data
   alice's email with one bit flipped: rejected, Unsupported state or unable to authenticate data
   check ok: the right ciphertext in the right place decrypts; moved, swapped or edited ciphertext is rejected

